> es of the general government (% of GDP, IMF GFS) by World Bank income group i
> n 1996; microstates excluded. The set of countries with data changes over tim
> e (Figure A1), which explains part of the year-to-year movement, especially i
> n the low-income group." _n _n

. file write `R' "**Figure 2. Public employment and relative public pay across 
> countries.**" _n _n "*Notes:* Country averages, 1996-2023. Horizontal axis: p
> ublic sector employment (% of total employment, WWBI). Vertical axis: general
>  government wage bill per public employee relative to GDP per worker. Labelle
> d countries selected for reference." _n _n

. file write `R' "**Figure 3. The denominator bias: the wage bill coefficient u
> nder different timings.**" _n _n "*Notes:* Coefficient of the wage bill in gr
> owth regressions that differ only in how the wage bill enters (Table A7). Ann
> ual estimates: country and year fixed effects, same sample, Driscoll-Kraay st
> andard errors. Five-year estimates: country and period fixed effects, standar
> d errors clustered by country. Bars are 95% confidence intervals." _n _n

. file write `R' "**Figure 4. Marginal effect of the wage bill on growth across
>  levels of meritocratic recruitment.**" _n _n "*Notes:* Solid line and shaded
>  band: marginal effect from the linear interaction model (Table 4, column 1) 
> with 95% confidence interval. Diamonds with bars: binning estimator at the me
